<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. B. van Heutsz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joannes Benedictus van Heutsz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">J. B. van Heutsz was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> general who brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a> to its decisive phase. From 1898, as military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-snouck-hurgronje">Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje</a>, he hunted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> with mobile Marechaussee units and forced the sultan's surrender in 1903. As governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> from 1904 to 1909 he completed Dutch control of the outer islands through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korte-verklaring">Short Declaration</a> and military expeditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-b-van-heutsz.jpg" alt="J. B. van Heutsz" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hannké · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_Benedictus_van_Heutsz_(1851-1924)._Gouverneur-generaal_(1904-09)_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-3814.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the advice of the Islamic scholar Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje, they eventually allied with the uleebalang against the ulama, and from 1898 General J.B. <b>van Heutsz</b> pursued the guerrillas with mobile units of the Marechaussee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The longest conflict was the Aceh War, begun in 1873, which turned into a guerrilla struggle; it was largely ended by mobile units of the Marechaussee under <b>J. B. van Heutsz</b>, with advice from the scholar Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje, by about 1904, though resistance continued for years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li></ul></section>
